=== The Command Line

There are a lot of different ways to use Git.
There are the original command-line tools, and there are many graphical user interfaces of varying capabilities.
For this book, we will be using Git on the command line.
For one, the command line is the only place you can run _all_ Git commands -- most of the GUIs implement only a partial subset of Git functionality for simplicity.
If you know how to run the command-line version, you can probably also figure out how to run the GUI version, while the opposite is not necessarily true.
Also, while your choice of graphical client is a matter of personal taste, _all_ users will have the command-line tools installed and available.

So we will expect you to know how to open Terminal in macOS or Command Prompt or PowerShell in Windows.
If you don't know what we're talking about here, you may need to stop and research that quickly so that you can follow the rest of the examples and descriptions in this book.

==== Visualizing Git State Transitions on the Command Line

When executing commands in the terminal, it is helpful to observe how Git transitions repository state across five components: the `HEAD` reference, branch references, commit ancestry, the index (staging area), and the working tree.

Consider a local repository initialized on branch `main` with three commits (A, B, and C) and a branch `topic` created at B:

[source,console]
----
$ git init -b main
$ git config user.name "Course Student"
$ git config user.email "student@example.invalid"

$ printf 'alpha\n' > notes.txt
$ git add notes.txt
$ git commit -m "A: create notes"

$ printf 'beta\n' >> notes.txt
$ git add notes.txt
$ git commit -m "B: append beta"
$ git branch topic

$ printf 'one\n' > todo.txt
$ git add todo.txt
$ git commit -m "C: add todo"
----

Before switching branches, inspect the current state on `main`:

[source,console]
----
$ git symbolic-ref HEAD
refs/heads/main
$ git rev-parse --short HEAD
<C>
$ git rev-parse --short topic
<B>
$ git status --short --branch
## main
$ git ls-files
notes.txt
todo.txt
----

The repository state before and after running `git checkout topic` is diagrammed below:

[source,text]
----
Before `git checkout topic`:
  HEAD (symbolic) --> refs/heads/main --> Commit C --> Commit B --> Commit A
                       refs/heads/topic --------------> Commit B --> Commit A
  Index (staged):     tree C [notes.txt, todo.txt]
  Working tree:       tree C [notes.txt, todo.txt]

After `git checkout topic`:
  HEAD (symbolic) --> refs/heads/topic --> Commit B --> Commit A
                       refs/heads/main ---------------> Commit C --> Commit B --> Commit A
  Index (staged):     tree B [notes.txt]
  Working tree:       tree B [notes.txt]
----

Now execute the branch switch command on the command line:

[source,console]
----
$ git checkout topic
Switched to branch 'topic'

$ git symbolic-ref HEAD
refs/heads/topic
$ git rev-parse --short HEAD
<B>
$ git rev-parse --short main
<C>
$ git status --short --branch
## topic
$ git ls-files
notes.txt
$ ls
notes.txt
----

The command-line operation produces the following state transitions:
* *Refs*: `HEAD` updates its symbolic pointer from `refs/heads/main` to `refs/heads/topic`. The branch references `main` (at C) and `topic` (at B) do not change their commit targets.
* *Commit ancestry*: The active history is now rooted at commit B (ancestry B -> A); commit C remains intact and reachable via `main`.
* *Index*: Git replaces the index tree with commit B's tree, removing `todo.txt` from the staging area.
* *Working tree*: Git updates the working directory to reflect commit B, removing `todo.txt` while retaining `notes.txt` ("alpha\nbeta\n").
* *Status*: `git status` confirms that the working tree and index are clean on branch `topic`.
